WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:08.193
As long ago as 1865, French writer Jules Verne told a tale of men journeying to the moon.

2
00:00:08.194 --> 00:00:14.961
In this story, From the Earth to the Moon , three men blast off from Florida in an aluminum

3
00:00:14.962 --> 00:00:19.666
capsule. They land on the moon and return to Earth by splashing down in the Pacific

4
00:00:19.667 --> 00:00:24.839
Ocean. Over one hundred years later, Verne's fiction became

5
00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:33.607
reality. In July 1969, three men in a spacecraft launched from Florida and landed on the moon in a mission called Apollo 11.

6
00:00:33.608 --> 00:00:37.613
When they returned, they splashed down in the Pacific Ocean.

7
00:00:37.614 --> 00:00:49.037
The big difference between these excursions is that the Apollo astronauts traveled in a rocket, but Verne's fictional travelers made the trip in a capsule shot from a cannon.

8
00:00:49.038 --> 00:00:55.839
Verne's story and others like it are examples of a prophetic genre of literature called science fiction.

9
00:00:55.840 --> 00:01:03.538
The work of its writers has often predicted future technologies and even influenced scientists making new inventions.

10
00:01:03.539 --> 00:01:11.713
Even though most science fiction is never realized, the best sci-fi, as it is also known, does have a way of coming true.

11
00:01:11.714 --> 00:01:15.361
In short, science fiction is about change.

12
00:01:15.362 --> 00:01:21.532
It envisions how the world will look down the road and describes how we might live with new technologies. Before

13
00:01:21.533 --> 00:01:31.862
the modern age, full of inventions and scientific discoveries, most people were born into a world that looked pretty much the same as the one in which their parents were raised. Interestingly

14
00:01:31.863 --> 00:01:39.589
enough, very few writers before the contemporary period produced anything that could be called sci-fi. That

15
00:01:39.590 --> 00:01:42.936
all ended in the nineteenth century. In

16
00:01:42.937 --> 00:01:50.772
1818, Mary Shelley published Frankenstein , one of the first major works of science fiction.

17
00:01:50.773 --> 00:01:57.298
In this story, a scientist assembles a creature from human body parts and brings it to life with electricity.

18
00:01:57.299 --> 00:02:04.149
Even today, the tale of Dr. Frankenstein and his monster is one of the best known science-fiction stories.

19
00:02:04.150 --> 00:02:11.276
Shelley's monster is still fictional, but Mary Shelley was right that electricity could be put to medical use.

20
00:02:11.277 --> 00:02:18.972
Today, doctors use electrical impulses to help people with amputations move their artificial legs and arms. Almost

21
00:02:18.973 --> 00:02:27.934
one hundred years after Frankenstein was published, British author H. G. Wells produced another major work that foretold the future. In

22
00:02:27.935 --> 00:02:35.592
his 1914 story The World Set Free , he wrote of atomic bombs well before they were created.

23
00:02:35.593 --> 00:02:45.681
Although his bombs worked very differently from the atomic bombs used by the U.S. in World War II, Wells accurately described their incredibly destructive power.

24
00:02:45.682 --> 00:02:53.341
Wells is also renowned for his 1898 novel The War of the Worlds , about an invasion from outer

25
00:02:53.342 --> 00:02:58.465
space. Thankfully, this plot has not come true yet—and likely never

26
00:02:58.466 --> 00:03:06.903
will. But people have become so fascinated with the idea of alien invaders from space that Wells' story has been retold many

27
00:03:06.904 --> 00:03:19.033
times. It has been adapted as a radio drama and three times as a movie, most recently as a Hollywood blockbuster by the same title, starring Tom Cruise and directed by Steven Spielberg.

28
00:03:19.034 --> 00:03:23.919
Wells was writing in the frightening time before the outbreak of World War I.

29
00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:27.971
The political atmosphere in Europe at this time was quite ominous.

30
00:03:27.972 --> 00:03:34.407
Nationalism was on the rise, with European nations racing to produce more and more armaments.

31
00:03:34.408 --> 00:03:36.399
War seemed inevitable.

32
00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:40.723
As a result, much of Wells' work tells of a depressing future.

33
00:03:40.724 --> 00:03:45.659
The work of the Czech writer Karel Čapek was also quite pessimistic.

34
00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:59.159
His play, R.U.R ., which stands for Rossem's Universal Robots , introduced the world to a word that has become not only a common theme in science fiction, but also a household

35
00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:02.549
term: "robot." The word comes from the Czech word for

36
00:04:02.550 --> 00:04:06.934
slave. In Čapek's play, the robots are meant to free people from

37
00:04:06.935 --> 00:04:10.555
work. Instead, they end up taking over the

38
00:04:10.556 --> 00:04:16.793
world. The famous science-fiction writer Isaac Asimov further popularized the idea of

39
00:04:16.794 --> 00:04:24.890
robots. Asimov's stories, first published mainly in magazines in the 1930s and 1940s, continue to attract millions of

40
00:04:24.891 --> 00:04:31.538
readers. In them, he gives his robots human form, a notion that has been imitated in hundreds of films and

41
00:04:31.539 --> 00:04:46.093
novels. This type of character has even inspired a Japanese scientist, Hiroshi Ishiguro, to create androids so lifelike that they can flutter their eyelids, move their arms and legs like a real person, and even appear to

42
00:04:46.094 --> 00:04:51.236
breathe! Another popular and prophetic science-fiction writer from that era is Arthur C.

43
00:04:51.237 --> 00:04:58.783
Clarke. In his 1945 novel Fountains of Paradise , Clarke was the first to propose the geostationary

44
00:04:58.784 --> 00:05:06.242
satellite. To be in geostationary orbit, a satellite must circle the earth at exactly the same speed as the earth

45
00:05:06.243 --> 00:05:12.225
rotates. As a result, the satellite appears to hover in a fixed position above the earth's

46
00:05:12.226 --> 00:05:15.952
surface. Clarke even worked out the necessary math for such a

47
00:05:15.953 --> 00:05:25.450
satellite. Almost twenty years later, on August 19, 1964, a satellite was launched into geostationary orbit for the first

48
00:05:25.451 --> 00:05:31.920
time. Many believe that the most influential work of science fiction from the past forty years is Star

49
00:05:31.921 --> 00:05:38.231
Trek. Star Trek was originally a television series that ran from 1966 to

50
00:05:38.232 --> 00:05:48.233
1969. Although it wasn't very popular when it was first broadcast, the show was loaded with innovations that would anticipate and inspire inventions for the next thirty years.

51
00:05:48.236 --> 00:05:57.861
For example, the "communicator" that the lead character, Captain James T. Kirk, used to speak with his crew was a palm-sized device that flipped open.

52
00:05:57.862 --> 00:06:02.796
Modern cell phones resemble the Star Trek communicator to a startling degree.

53
00:06:02.797 --> 00:06:07.571
Star Trek was also among the first shows to feature talking computers.

54
00:06:07.572 --> 00:06:15.833
The onboard navigation systems in today's luxury cars may not be as complex, but they serve the same function as the ones on the show.

55
00:06:15.834 --> 00:06:24.072
Years later, Star Trek 's popularity skyrocketed when the original cast reunited to make a series of successful

56
00:06:24.073 --> 00:06:29.609
movies. Perhaps Star Trek 's greatest contribution was its reflection of changing

57
00:06:29.610 --> 00:06:35.167
social norms. In the future envisioned by Star Trek creator Gene Roddenberry, the world was

58
00:06:35.168 --> 00:06:42.024
without racism. As a result, he gave prominent roles to African-American and Asian actors, among other

59
00:06:42.025 --> 00:06:52.409
nationalities. The African American who played communications officer Lt. Uhura, Nichelle Nichols, wanted to leave the program after the first season because she was bored with her

60
00:06:52.410 --> 00:07:03.763
role. She stayed because Martin Luther King Jr., the heroic civil rights leader of the 1960s, convinced her that her character was a revolutionary role model for young African-American

61
00:07:03.764 --> 00:07:08.276
women. Science-fiction writers continue to influence science and

62
00:07:08.277 --> 00:07:15.982
society. The writer Carl Sagan, for example, worked for the United States government in several positions, ranging from a researcher to a

63
00:07:15.983 --> 00:07:26.151
consultant. It was he who convinced NASA, the American agency in charge of the space program, to include messages on the two Voyager spacecraft launched in the

64
00:07:26.152 --> 00:07:32.928
1970s. The messages give information about humans and location of Earth in case the ships are discovered by extraterrestrials.

65
00:07:32.929 --> 00:07:42.859
Few scientists have had the chance to influence the actions of a government quite this much, and some complain that writers and entertainers should not be advising politicians.

66
00:07:42.860 --> 00:07:53.410
For fans of science fiction, however, the future imagined by its best writers is inspirational, not just for new technologies, but also for society and human progress.

67
00:07:53.411 --> 00:07:57.070
It's hard to imagine a future without science fiction.

